paper/main.tex:33:Does adding context hurt a small language model, and do the location and composition of that context matter? We evaluate the frozen \modelName{} artifact on \sampleSize{} synthetic contract-clause probes. Deterministic runs span mean prompt lengths of \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Moving the same answer-bearing clause from the start to the end raised accuracy by \positionTwoStartEndChange{} percentage points at the shortest tier and \positionFourStartEndChange{} points at the middle tier, while keyword pruning changed matched accuracy by \pruningChange{} points. Removing only the lexical decoy from each pruned prompt then changed accuracy by \pruningNoDecoyChange{} points. The matched comparison across prompt lengths was inconclusive: accuracy rose by \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points and then changed by \amountSharedFourEightChange{} points, with only \amountSharedEightCount{} shared cases at each tier. Code-heavy business filler reduced accuracy relative to unrelated filler, but the manipulation does not isolate topical relevance from its other differences.
paper/main.tex:44:We provide a paired evaluation of a frozen open model at laptop scale. Prior work established position and length failures in larger systems \citep{liu2023lost,levy2024same} and showed that irrelevant statements can disrupt reasoning \citep{shi2023distracted}. Our comparisons match prompt lengths, answer positions, and questions while varying whether the filler is code-heavy business text or unrelated prose. We also apply a deterministic keyword filter to the same cases evaluated with the full prompt.
paper/main.tex:51:    \item Does keyword pruning recover accuracy, and can answer removal explain its failures?
paper/main.tex:54:The experiment does not identify a general context-length threshold. Within this grid, accuracy was higher when the answer appeared near the end and lower with code-heavy business filler than with unrelated prose. Keyword pruning amplified the lexical trap built into the probes.
paper/main.tex:58:\citet{liu2023lost} found a U-shaped position curve in multi-document question answering and key-value retrieval, with stronger use of information near context boundaries. We conduct a smaller clause-reordering test in which the question, filler sequence, clauses, and codes remain fixed while we insert the answer clause at a different depth. When we place it before the competing clause, the competing clause's final sentence index shifts by one. The resulting pattern mainly favors recent information rather than forming a full U-shape, because accuracy is also low in the start condition.
paper/main.tex:62:\citet{shi2023distracted} added irrelevant statements to arithmetic problems and found lower reasoning accuracy. We compare filler from the same business domain with unrelated prose while matching prompt length and question wording. The business condition also contains more entities and code-shaped candidates, so the comparison measures that bundle rather than topical relevance alone. NoLiMa shows that synthetic retrieval tests based on literal word matches can overstate retrieval ability \citep{modarressi2025nolima}. We therefore paraphrase the question in the answer clause while giving the competing clause the same surface wording as the question. The tested model belongs to the instruction-tuned Qwen family \citep{qwen2024qwen25}.
paper/main.tex:72:For each family, seeded code generates the target company and code, a competing company and code, a question, and filler. The question asks for the renewal code needed to extend the target company's contract. Its wording exactly matches the competing clause, whereas the answer-bearing clause calls the same value a renewal identifier. A matched literal control reverses the wording of the two clauses while retaining the same filler.
paper/main.tex:74:For each full-context family, we insert the answer clause near the start, middle, or end. Family generation does not use target position in any random seed. We assign the competing clause a fractional insertion index, either earlier or later in the document, that is independent of answer position and fixed across the three versions. Its final sentence index shifts by one when the answer is inserted before it. Thus, each paired position contrast changes both the answer placement and this mechanical one-sentence shift while preserving the scenario content and filler order.
paper/main.tex:84:At the middle tier, a keyword filter retains sentences with at least two content words shared with the question, falling back to the highest-overlap sentences if too few qualify. It preserves original order. The comparison is restricted to the \pruningDenominator{} cases with both full and pruned outputs. We also include \closedBookCount{} questions asked without the document (closed-book questions) and \literalControlDenominator{} matched literal-versus-paraphrase controls.
paper/main.tex:86:The filter retained the answer clause in \pruningTargetRetainedCount{} of \pruningPrunedCount{} matched prompts. It retained the lexical decoy in \pruningDistractorRetainedCount{} of them. A paired diagnostic removes only that decoy from each pruned prompt, leaving the answer clause and every other selected sentence fixed. This control uses the known synthetic label to diagnose the filter; it is not a deployable retrieval method.
paper/main.tex:90:Normalized accuracy requires the target code to appear without the competing code. Exact-output compliance requires a response containing one code and no other text. A distractor capture contains the competing code without the target, and an invalid response contains both expected codes or neither. We strip only the runner's fixed transport trailer before applying these rules.
paper/main.tex:165:\textbf{Keyword pruning reduced accuracy on the matched cases.} Full-context accuracy was \pruningFullAccuracy{}\%, compared with \pruningPrunedAccuracy{}\% after filtering, a change of \pruningChange{} points [\pruningChangeLow{}, \pruningChangeHigh{}] across \pruningFamilyCount{} families. The paired comparison had \pruningWins{} win, \pruningLosses{} losses, and \pruningTies{} ties (Table~\ref{tab:pruning}). Because every pruned prompt retained the answer clause, answer removal cannot explain this decline.
paper/main.tex:168:\caption{Matched keyword-pruning result and diagnostic controls. Accuracy intervals are \confidenceLevel{}\% family-cluster bootstrap intervals.}
