    26	\icmlcorrespondingauthor{Anonymous Author}{anon.email@domain.com}
    27	\icmlkeywords{long context, retrieval, position effects, small language models}
    28	\vskip 0.3in
    29	]
    30	\printAffiliationsAndNotice{}
    31	
    32	\begin{abstract}
    33	Does adding context hurt a small language model, and do the location and composition of that context matter? We evaluate the frozen \modelName{} artifact on \sampleSize{} synthetic contract-clause probes. Deterministic runs span mean prompt lengths of \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Moving the same answer-bearing clause from the start to the end raised accuracy by \positionTwoStartEndChange{} percentage points at the shortest tier and \positionFourStartEndChange{} points at the middle tier, while keyword pruning changed matched accuracy by \pruningChange{} points. Removing only the lexical decoy from each pruned prompt then changed accuracy by \pruningNoDecoyChange{} points. The matched comparison across prompt lengths was inconclusive: accuracy rose by \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points and then changed by \amountSharedFourEightChange{} points, with only \amountSharedEightCount{} shared cases at each tier. Code-heavy business filler reduced accuracy relative to unrelated filler, but the manipulation does not isolate topical relevance from its other differences.
    34	\end{abstract}
    35	
    36	\section{Introduction}
    37	
    38	You paste a long contract into a chatbot and ask, ``What code renews the vendor agreement?'' The answer sits in one clause, but a similar buyer clause repeats the words in your question and gives a different code.
    39	
    40	This ordinary lookup becomes less predictable in a long prompt. The model may fail because it must process a longer document, because the answer appears in a weak position, or because nearby text resembles the question. Each explanation suggests a different response: shorten the prompt, move the key clause, or filter the document. Distinguishing among them requires an evaluation that varies a single property while holding the others fixed.
    41	
    42	We study these choices in a synthetic, gradeable version of the contract situation. Each document contains an answer-bearing vendor clause and a look-alike clause for another company. The question uses the wording of the look-alike clause, while the answer clause expresses the same relation with different words. This construction reduces the value of literal matching, following the motivation of NoLiMa \citep{modarressi2025nolima}.
    43	
    44	We provide a paired evaluation of a frozen open model at laptop scale. Prior work established position and length failures in larger systems \citep{liu2023lost,levy2024same} and showed that irrelevant statements can disrupt reasoning \citep{shi2023distracted}. Our comparisons match prompt lengths, answer positions, and questions while varying whether the filler is code-heavy business text or unrelated prose. We also apply a deterministic keyword filter to the same cases evaluated with the full prompt.
    45	
    46	The experiment asks four questions:
    47	\begin{enumerate}
    48	    \item As mean prompt length grows from \tierTwoTokens{} to \tierEightTokens{} tokens, how much does accuracy change on shared cases?
    49	    \item How does moving the same answer clause among the start, middle, and end change accuracy?
    50	    \item How does accuracy differ between code-heavy business filler and unrelated prose in matched documents?
    51	    \item Does keyword pruning recover accuracy, and can answer removal explain its failures?
    52	\end{enumerate}
    53	
    54	The experiment does not identify a general context-length threshold. Within this grid, accuracy was higher when the answer appeared near the end and lower with code-heavy business filler than with unrelated prose. Keyword pruning amplified the lexical trap built into the probes.
    55	
    56	\section{Related Work}
    57	
    58	\citet{liu2023lost} found a U-shaped position curve in multi-document question answering and key-value retrieval, with stronger use of information near context boundaries. We conduct a smaller clause-reordering test in which the question, filler sequence, clauses, and codes remain fixed while we insert the answer clause at a different depth. When we place it before the competing clause, the competing clause's final sentence index shifts by one. The resulting pattern mainly favors recent information rather than forming a full U-shape, because accuracy is also low in the start condition.
    59	
    60	Prompt length can matter even when the underlying problem is fixed. \citet{levy2024same} padded matched reasoning problems and observed degradation well before model context limits, while RULER found that usable context lengths can be shorter than advertised windows \citep{hsieh2024ruler}. Our prompt-length comparison includes only cases present at every tier, so the mix of scenarios remains fixed as prompts grow.
    61	
    62	\citet{shi2023distracted} added irrelevant statements to arithmetic problems and found lower reasoning accuracy. We compare filler from the same business domain with unrelated prose while matching prompt length and question wording. The business condition also contains more entities and code-shaped candidates, so the comparison measures that bundle rather than topical relevance alone. NoLiMa shows that synthetic retrieval tests based on literal word matches can overstate retrieval ability \citep{modarressi2025nolima}. We therefore paraphrase the question in the answer clause while giving the competing clause the same surface wording as the question. The tested model belongs to the instruction-tuned Qwen family \citep{qwen2024qwen25}.
    63	
    64	\section{Probe Design}
    65	
    66	\paragraph{Model and execution.}
    67	We evaluate the frozen \modelFile{} artifact, whose SHA checksum appears below:
    68	\par\noindent\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{\modelSha}\par
    69	We run it through \texttt{llama-completion} with greedy decoding, zero temperature, and a fixed seed. The complete manifest contains \manifestProbeCount{} probes, each stored as one prompt and one raw output. Existing outputs are skipped on rerun, so interrupted inference resumes by stable probe identifier.
    70	
    71	\paragraph{Scenario families.}
    72	For each family, seeded code generates the target company and code, a competing company and code, a question, and filler. The question asks for the renewal code needed to extend the target company's contract. Its wording exactly matches the competing clause, whereas the answer-bearing clause calls the same value a renewal identifier. A matched literal control reverses the wording of the two clauses while retaining the same filler.
    73	
    74	For each full-context family, we insert the answer clause near the start, middle, or end. Family generation does not use target position in any random seed. We assign the competing clause a fractional insertion index, either earlier or later in the document, that is independent of answer position and fixed across the three versions. Its final sentence index shifts by one when the answer is inserted before it. Thus, each paired position contrast changes both the answer placement and this mechanical one-sentence shift while preserving the scenario content and filler order.
    75	
    76	\paragraph{Amount and filler composition.}
    77	The tokenizer, rather than a character estimate, determines prompt length. The three full-context tiers average \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Each tier balances business filler with unrelated sentences. Business filler consists of other companies, records, and unrelated codes; unrelated filler describes places and events outside the contract domain. Filler pairs share the family, target position, question, and measured tier.
    78	
    79	This manipulation changes several properties together. Compared with unrelated prose, the related filler contains more business entities, contract-like templates, and code-shaped answer candidates. The paired comparison therefore identifies the effect of these two generated filler conditions, not the effect of topical relevance alone.
    80	
    81	The final tier contains fewer families because it is more expensive to run. Prompt-length estimates therefore use only the \amountSharedEightCount{} cases shared by every tier rather than the unequal full-tier totals. Filler-composition estimates use \qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{}, \qualityFourRelevantDenominator{}, and \qualityEightRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the three tiers, respectively.
    82	
    83	\paragraph{Pruning and controls.}
    84	At the middle tier, a keyword filter retains sentences with at least two content words shared with the question, falling back to the highest-overlap sentences if too few qualify. It preserves original order. The comparison is restricted to the \pruningDenominator{} cases with both full and pruned outputs. We also include \closedBookCount{} questions asked without the document (closed-book questions) and \literalControlDenominator{} matched literal-versus-paraphrase controls.
    85	
    86	The filter retained the answer clause in \pruningTargetRetainedCount{} of \pruningPrunedCount{} matched prompts. It retained the lexical decoy in \pruningDistractorRetainedCount{} of them. A paired diagnostic removes only that decoy from each pruned prompt, leaving the answer clause and every other selected sentence fixed. This control uses the known synthetic label to diagnose the filter; it is not a deployable retrieval method.
    87	
    88	\section{Scoring and Comparisons}
    89	
    90	Normalized accuracy requires the target code to appear without the competing code. Exact-output compliance requires a response containing one code and no other text. A distractor capture contains the competing code without the target, and an invalid response contains both expected codes or neither. We strip only the runner's fixed transport trailer before applying these rules.
    91	
    92	The inferential target is the population of synthetic scenario families defined by the seeded generator. To compute each \confidenceLevel{}\% interval, we resample whole observed families while retaining all positions, filler types, and variants from every selected family. Exact enumeration makes this cluster bootstrap deterministic. Paired changes are differences in accuracy over an exact intersection of scenario keys, and their intervals use the same family-level resampling. The intervals describe sensitivity to the small sample of families, while wins, losses, and ties expose the underlying paired outcomes. Because greedy decoding is deterministic, the intervals do not measure uncertainty from repeated decoding.
    93	
    94	\section{Results}
    95	
    96	\paragraph{Amount.}
    97	\textbf{The relationship between prompt length and accuracy remains unresolved.} Accuracy was \amountSharedTwoAccuracy{}\% at a mean prompt length of \tierTwoTokens{} tokens and \amountSharedFourAccuracy{}\% at \tierFourTokens{} tokens. This paired change of \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points has a family-cluster interval of [\amountSharedTwoFourChangeLow{}, \amountSharedTwoFourChangeHigh{}] over \amountSharedTwoFourDenominator{} cases. At \tierEightTokens{} tokens, accuracy was \amountSharedEightAccuracy{}\%, with \amountSharedFourEightWins{} wins, \amountSharedFourEightLosses{} losses, and \amountSharedFourEightTies{} ties relative to the middle tier (Table~\ref{tab:amount-quality}). Because the longest-tier intersection represents only \amountSharedEightCount{} probes from \amountSharedEightFamilyCount{} scenario families, these observations neither establish a benefit nor rule out degradation within the measured range.
    98	
    99	\begin{table}[t]
   100	\caption{Matched amount and filler-composition comparisons. Brackets give \confidenceLevel{}\% family-cluster bootstrap intervals; $n$ is the matched denominator. Changes are later minus earlier for amount and business filler minus unrelated prose for composition.}
