   157	\end{tabular}
   158	}
   159	\end{table}
   160	
   161	\paragraph{Filler composition.}
   162	\textbf{Code-heavy business filler produced lower accuracy than unrelated prose.} The matched difference was \qualityTwoRelevantChange{} points [\qualityTwoRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityTwoRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the shortest tier and \qualityFourRelevantChange{} points [\qualityFourRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityFourRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityFourRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the middle tier. At the shortest tier, the paired outcomes were \qualityTwoRelevantWins{} win, \qualityTwoRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityTwoRelevantTies{} ties. At the middle tier, they were \qualityFourRelevantWins{} win, \qualityFourRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityFourRelevantTies{} ties. The longest-tier estimate was \qualityEightRelevantChange{} points, but its \qualityEightRelevantFamilyCount{} families are too sparse to strengthen that conclusion. Because the business filler also adds code-shaped candidates and repeated entity templates, these results do not show that topical relevance caused the difference.
   163	
   164	\paragraph{Mitigation.}
   165	\textbf{Keyword pruning reduced accuracy on the matched cases.} Full-context accuracy was \pruningFullAccuracy{}\%, compared with \pruningPrunedAccuracy{}\% after filtering, a change of \pruningChange{} points [\pruningChangeLow{}, \pruningChangeHigh{}] across \pruningFamilyCount{} families. The paired comparison had \pruningWins{} win, \pruningLosses{} losses, and \pruningTies{} ties (Table~\ref{tab:pruning}). Because every pruned prompt retained the answer clause, answer removal cannot explain this decline.
